### Forecast
**Prediction:** NO
**Confidence:** 0.82
**Reasoning:**

**Structural assessment:** The Cuban regime has survived 67 years (since 1959) through: collapse of the Soviet Union (1991, loss of $4-6B annual subsidies), the Special Period (1990s economic depression), multiple US administrations attempting to isolate it, Fidel Castro's death (2016), and the COVID-19 pandemic. The PCC's monopoly on power is enforced through state security (Ministry of Interior), the Committees for the Defense of the Revolution (CDRs, neighborhood-level surveillance), and control over all legal economic activity.

**Current pressure points:** (1) Economic crisis — severe shortages of food, medicine, fuel since 2021, exacerbated by post-COVID tourism collapse and reduced Venezuelan oil subsidies; (2) Emigration wave — largest exodus since Mariel boatlift (1980), with ~500K+ Cubans leaving since 2022, primarily through Nicaragua and US border; (3) Aging leadership — Díaz-Canel (born 1960) is the first non-Castro leader since 1959 but lacks the revolutionary legitimacy of the Castro brothers; (4) July 2021 protests were the largest since 1994 — regime responded with mass arrests (1,000+) and internet shutdowns, demonstrating willingness to suppress.

**Why regime survival remains likely for 2026:** (1) No organized opposition — the PCC is the only legal party; dissidents are systematically monitored, imprisoned, or exiled; (2) Security apparatus loyalty — the Ministry of Interior (MININT) and Revolutionary Armed Forces (FAR) remain under PCC control with no history of factional splits; (3) No external intervention scenario — the US is in diplomatic mode (Iran peace deal, Colombia election monitoring) with no appetite for Cuba intervention; (4) Regime-change base rates in one-party communist states: Soviet Union (74 years), China (75+ years and counting), North Korea (75+), Vietnam (48+ years since reunification). Cuba at 67 years is within the durable range; (5) Emigration acts as a pressure valve — discontented Cubans leave rather than organize domestically, reducing revolutionary pressure.

**Tail risk (~18%):** (1) Díaz-Canel health crisis or death → succession uncertainty (Raúl Castro is 95, Miguel Díaz-Canel has no clear successor); (2) Economic collapse triggering July 2021-scale protests that security forces refuse to suppress; (3) US policy shift toward active regime-change support (unlikely given current diplomatic focus); (4) Major natural disaster (hurricane) overwhelming state capacity during ongoing economic crisis.

**Market alignment:** 18% YES is appropriately priced. It reflects the tail risk of a sudden leadership vacuum or economic collapse without overpricing a structured regime-change probability. My confidence of 0.82 aligns with the market's 0.82 NO price.

